        {
            "block_label": "header",
            "block_content": "Style: Lessons in Clarity and Grace",
            "block_bbox": [
                164,
                69,
                421,
                91
            ],
            "block_id": 1,
            "block_order": null,
            "group_id": 1,
            "global_block_id": 1,
            "global_group_id": 1,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    164.0,
                    69.0
                ],
                [
                    421.0,
                    69.0
                ],
                [
                    421.0,
                    91.0
                ],
                [
                    164.0,
                    91.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Linguist Deborah Tannen suggests that our “argument culture” is based on our tendency to regard different positions and concepts as “continually at war,” and she challenges us to seek a “balance” instead (284).",
            "block_bbox": [
                126,
                116,
                754,
                191
            ],
            "block_id": 2,
            "block_order": 1,
            "group_id": 2,
            "global_block_id": 2,
            "global_group_id": 2,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    126.0,
                    116.0
                ],
                [
                    754.0,
                    116.0
                ],
                [
                    754.0,
                    191.0
                ],
                [
                    126.0,
                    191.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "The fewer words you quote, the more emphasis they receive. But you sacrifice the context of the original and limit your readers' ability to verify for themselves your interpretation of your source. When you quote isolated words or short phrases, you implicitly ask your readers to trust that you are representing your source accurately and fairly.",
            "block_bbox": [
                90,
                208,
                785,
                337
            ],
            "block_id": 3,
            "block_order": 2,
            "group_id": 3,
            "global_block_id": 3,
            "global_group_id": 3,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    90.0,
                    208.0
                ],
                [
                    785.0,
                    208.0
                ],
                [
                    785.0,
                    337.0
                ],
                [
                    90.0,
                    337.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Use just a few words from a source. When you repeat just a few words from a source, you have to decide whether or not to quote them. If they are words anyone might use, treat them as your own. If they are strikingly original or especially important, put them in quotation marks and cite their source. Consider that first sentence from Tannen:",
            "block_bbox": [
                90,
                349,
                785,
                478
            ],
            "block_id": 4,
            "block_order": 3,
            "group_id": 4,
            "global_block_id": 4,
            "global_group_id": 4,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    90.0,
                    349.0
                ],
                [
                    785.0,
                    349.0
                ],
                [
                    785.0,
                    478.0
                ],
                [
                    90.0,
                    478.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "At the heart of the argument culture is our habit of seeing issues and ideas as absolute and irreconcilable principles continually at war.",
            "block_bbox": [
                123,
                491,
                755,
                540
            ],
            "block_id": 5,
            "block_order": 4,
            "group_id": 5,
            "global_block_id": 5,
            "global_group_id": 5,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    123.0,
                    491.0
                ],
                [
                    755.0,
                    491.0
                ],
                [
                    755.0,
                    540.0
                ],
                [
                    123.0,
                    540.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "The phrase “issues and ideas” is so ordinary that it requires neither citation nor quotation marks. The term “argument culture,” though, is Tannen’s coinage and should be quoted and cited. Once you cite those words, you can use them again without quotation marks or citation.",
            "block_bbox": [
                89,
                555,
                785,
                659
            ],
            "block_id": 6,
            "block_order": 5,
            "group_id": 6,
            "global_block_id": 6,
            "global_group_id": 6,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    89.0,
                    555.0
                ],
                [
                    785.0,
                    555.0
                ],
                [
                    785.0,
                    659.0
                ],
                [
                    89.0,
                    659.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "paragraph_title",
            "block_content": "How to Attribute a Source: Explicitly or Not?",
            "block_bbox": [
                91,
                677,
                578,
                705
            ],
            "block_id": 7,
            "block_order": 6,
            "group_id": 7,
            "global_block_id": 7,
            "global_group_id": 7,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    91.0,
                    677.0
                ],
                [
                    578.0,
                    677.0
                ],
                [
                    578.0,
                    705.0
                ],
                [
                    91.0,
                    705.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "When summarizing, paraphrasing, or quoting, you have to choose whether or not to name your source's author explicitly. Compare the following:",
            "block_bbox": [
                92,
                713,
                783,
                765
            ],
            "block_id": 8,
            "block_order": 7,
            "group_id": 8,
            "global_block_id": 8,
            "global_group_id": 8,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    92.0,
                    713.0
                ],
                [
                    783.0,
                    713.0
                ],
                [
                    783.0,
                    765.0
                ],
                [
                    92.0,
                    765.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Linguist Deborah Tannen observes that our society's approach to argument is based on an assumption that competing positions and ideas must always be in conflict (284).",
            "block_bbox": [
                125,
                776,
                755,
                851
            ],
            "block_id": 9,
            "block_order": 8,
            "group_id": 9,
            "global_block_id": 9,
            "global_group_id": 9,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    125.0,
                    776.0
                ],
                [
                    755.0,
                    776.0
                ],
                [
                    755.0,
                    851.0
                ],
                [
                    125.0,
                    851.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Our society's approach to argument is based on an assumption that competing positions and ideas must always be in conflict (Tannen 284).",
            "block_bbox": [
                126,
                868,
                754,
                918
            ],
            "block_id": 10,
            "block_order": 9,
            "group_id": 10,
            "global_block_id": 10,
            "global_group_id": 10,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    126.0,
                    868.0
                ],
                [
                    754.0,
                    868.0
                ],
                [
                    754.0,
                    918.0
                ],
                [
                    126.0,
                    918.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Which you should choose depends on your purpose. When you explicitly attribute an idea to another writer, as in the first version, you hold it at arm's length. When you acknowledge an author only parenthetically, as in the second version, you seem to embrace her idea as your own. If you want to agree with Tannen, you could choose either version:",
            "block_bbox": [
                91,
                934,
                785,
                1064
            ],
            "block_id": 11,
            "block_order": 10,
            "group_id": 11,
            "global_block_id": 11,
            "global_group_id": 11,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    89.88318634033203,
                    934.0096435546875
                ],
                [
                    782.8831176757812,
                    928.0096435546875
                ],
